  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1108 AM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWeak monsoonal moisture will continue to work beneath the ridge\nof high pressure centered over the region. Showers and\nthunderstorms will be much more isolated today and Tuesday with\nlighter moisture, favoring the terrain immediately west of the \nDivide. Drier air at the surface in our lower valleys will\nremain fertile for gusty outflow winds beneath decaying storms.\nThese isolated storms will pose a lightning threat as well,\ndespite the sunny skies in the valleys. This elevates the dry\nlightning threat and potential for new fire starts. Moisture \nbegins to deepen on Wednesday and Thursday. This will increase \nprecipitation chances around the region, with some wetting rains\nexpected both days as the monsoonal flow awakens from its \nslumber. Ensemble models are advertising PWAT values in the 140 \npercent of normal range. While this isn't particularly \nimpressive, it provides enough hope for wetting rains to break \nthis hot and dry pattern. Widespread flooding is not expected, \nbut a few localized showers could pose headaches for flashy \nterrain and burn scars.\n\nThe influx of moisture will also help with moderating afternoon\ntemperatures. Highs will trend above normal this afternoon and\nTuesday. Increasing cloud cover and outflows from mountain\nshowers will begin to work on some of our warmer valleys on\nWednesday, and to a greater extent, Thursday and beyond. While a\nbig push of monsoonal moisture is not expected in the extended\nperiod. There is some good news in the persistence of this\nweaker moisture. This forecast will likely ebb and flow over the\nnext week. Afternoon showers and thunderstorms will continue on\nthe mountains and over some valleys. Should we see some repeat\nshowers in favored locations, this could lend itself to a\ngreater flash flood risk. Outdoor enthusiasts and residents of\nnearby burn scars will want to stay abreast of changing forecast\nconditions as we move back out of the hot, dry pattern.",
